# Cookies

<p class="text-muted-foreground text-sm">{formattedDate}</p>

There was supposed to be an article here, but everything has [already been said](https://www-test.ics.uci.edu/~fielding/pubs/dissertation/evaluation.htm#sec_6_3_4_2).

Cookies are evil. They serve no purpose other than violating privacy and compromising security.

<details class="group mt-12">
  <summary class="text-muted-foreground flex cursor-pointer text-sm list-none items-center gap-1 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
    Vulnerabilities introduced by cookies
    <ChevronDown class="size-4 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" />
  </summary>
  <ol class="timeline">
    <li>
      <time>1994</time>
      <div><strong>Supercookies</strong>The original dot-counting rule let any site set a cookie for all of <code>.co.uk</code>. Patched by the Public Suffix List (2007), which is maintained by hand.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>1996</time>
      <div><strong>Cross-site tracking</strong>Third-party cookies follow the user across sites. Blocked by Safari (2020), partitioned by Firefox (2022); Chrome gave up in 2024.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2000</time>
      <div><strong>Cookie theft via XSS</strong>Any script on the page reads <code>document.cookie</code>. Patched by <code>HttpOnly</code> (2002), opt-in.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2001</time>
      <div><strong>CSRF</strong>Any site makes the browser send an authenticated request. Patched by tokens in every framework, then <code>SameSite</code> (2016), on by default since 2020.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2002</time>
      <div><strong>Session fixation</strong>The attacker plants a known session ID before login. Patched in application code.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2003</time>
      <div><strong>Cross-site tracing</strong>The <code>TRACE</code> method echoes the cookie back to a script, bypassing <code>HttpOnly</code>. Browsers banned <code>TRACE</code>.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2006</time>
      <div><strong>XSSI and JSONP leaks</strong>A <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag pointing to another origin carries cookies and leaks the authenticated response. Patched by unparseable prefixes, then CORB (2018).</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2007</time>
      <div><strong>Cross-site leaks</strong>Timing, errors, and frame counts of authenticated cross-site responses reveal the user's state. Mitigated by Fetch Metadata (2019), CORP, and COOP (2020), all opt-in.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2008</time>
      <div><strong>Clickjacking</strong>A framed page is still authenticated, so a disguised click acts as the user. Patched by <code>X-Frame-Options</code> (2009), opt-in.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2008</time>
      <div><strong>Login CSRF</strong>The attacker logs the victim into the attacker's account. Patched in application code.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2010</time>
      <div><strong>Client-side trust</strong>State kept in a cookie is attacker-controlled input: ASP.NET's encrypted cookies fell to a padding oracle, unsigned and serialized ones are simply edited. Patched in application code.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2010</time>
      <div><strong>Sidejacking</strong>Firesheep hijacks sessions on open Wi-Fi with one click. <code>Secure</code> had existed since 1994, unused. Patched by HSTS (2012), opt-in.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2011–2014</time>
      <div><strong>BEAST, CRIME, BREACH, POODLE</strong>The same secret is resent with every request, so it can be guessed byte by byte. Patched by removing TLS compression and SSL 3.0; BREACH is only mitigated.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2013</time>
      <div><strong>Cookie tossing</strong>A subdomain or a plain HTTP response overwrites the cookies of an HTTPS site. Patched by the <code>__Host-</code> prefix (2016), opt-in by cookie name.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2013</time>
      <div><strong>Cross-site WebSocket hijacking</strong>The handshake carries cookies and is exempt from the same-origin policy. Patched in application code.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2014</time>
      <div><strong>Cookie bomb</strong>Oversized cookies make the server reject every request. Not patched.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2016</time>
      <div><strong>CORS with credentials</strong>A server that reflects <code>Origin</code> and allows credentials lets any site read authenticated responses. Patched in application code.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>now</time>
      <div><strong>Session replay</strong>A cookie is a bearer token: malware copies it and skips the second factor. Device Bound Session Credentials shipped in Chrome for Windows in 2026.</div>
    </li>
  </ol>
</details>

